= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Seek a positive weight such that the <differential operator> becomes $-w^{-1}(py')'$. Matching the second <derivative> gives $p=w(1-x^2)$, while matching the first <derivative> requires $p'=-xw$. Consequently
$$
(1-x^2)w'-2xw=-xw,\qquad \frac{w'}w=\frac{x}{1-x^2}.
$$
Choosing the multiplicative constant to be one gives
$$
\boxed{w(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{1-x^2}},\qquad p(x)=\sqrt{1-x^2},\qquad -(py_n')'=n^2wy_n}.
$$
This is <Sturm-Liouville form> with zero potential term. Multiply the equations for $y_n,y_m$ by $y_m,y_n$, subtract, and integrate. The <Lagrange identity for a Sturm-Liouville operator> gives
$$
(n^2-m^2)\int_{-1}^1wy_ny_m\,dx
=\left[p(y_ny_m'-y_my_n')\right]_{-1}^{1}=0.
$$
The boundary expression vanishes because $p\to0$ and the functions and their <derivatives> are finite. Since $n,m\geq0$ are distinct, their <eigenvalues> $n^2,m^2$ are distinct, proving
$$
\boxed{\int_{-1}^{1}\frac{y_n(x)y_m(x)}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx=0\quad(n\ne m)}.
$$
The endpoint singularities of the weight are integrable, so these <weighted inner products> exist.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $n=0$, a constant solution and its normalization give $y_0=1$. For $n=1$, substitute $y=ax+b$: the equation gives $b=0$, and the normalization gives $a=1$. For $n=2$, put $y=ax^2+bx+c$. Comparing coefficients gives $b=0$ and $c=-a/2$; then $y(1)=1$ fixes $a=2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y_0=1,\qquad y_1=x,\qquad y_2=2x^2-1}.
$$
These are the first three <Chebyshev polynomials>. For their squared <weighted norms>, use $x=\cos\theta$, which converts $dx/\sqrt{1-x^2}$ into $-d\theta$:
$$
\boxed{I_0=\int_0^\pi1\,d\theta=\pi,\qquad
I_1=\int_0^\pi\cos^2\theta\,d\theta=\frac\pi2}.
$$
More generally, $y_n(\cos\theta)=\cos(n\theta)$ makes their <orthogonality> the usual <cosine> <orthogonality>.

= c
{parent=1}
