= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $L=-(1-x^2)D^2+xD$, so $Ly_n=n^2y_n$ and the forced equation is $(\gamma^2-L)Z=e^{\varepsilon x}$. The small-parameter <Taylor expansion> of the forcing is
$$
e^{\varepsilon x}=1+\varepsilon x+\frac{\varepsilon^2x^2}{2}+O(\varepsilon^3)
=y_0+\varepsilon y_1+\frac{\varepsilon^2}{4}(y_0+y_2)+O(\varepsilon^3).
$$
Each <Chebyshev polynomial> can be inverted separately under $\gamma^2-L$. None of the required denominators vanishes because $\gamma$ is a real noninteger. Thus the <regular solution of a forced Chebyshev equation> has
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{y_0}{\gamma^2},\qquad
B=\frac{y_1}{\gamma^2-1},\qquad
C=\frac{y_0}{4\gamma^2}+\frac{y_2}{4(\gamma^2-4)}}.
$$
Here we select the same regular endpoint domain as in part (a), with finite function values and finite endpoint <derivatives>. This qualification is needed: the printed forced equation alone does not state endpoint conditions. Without this selection one can add $c_1\cos(\gamma\arccos x)+c_2\sin(\gamma\arccos x)$, which remain finite but generally have singular endpoint <derivatives>. With $x=\cos\theta$, regularity gives $Z_\theta(0)=Z_\theta(\pi)=0$; the corresponding homogeneous equation has only the zero solution for noninteger $\gamma$. This makes the displayed expansion unique.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Write the integral's multiplier in the <Chebyshev polynomial> basis:
$$
(1-x^2)^{-1/2}+(1-x^2)^{1/2}
=w(2-x^2)=w\left(\frac32y_0-\frac12y_2\right).
$$
At $\gamma^2=5$, part (i) gives
$$
Z=\left(\frac15+\frac{\varepsilon^2}{20}\right)y_0
+\frac\varepsilon4y_1+\frac{\varepsilon^2}{4}y_2+O(\varepsilon^3).
$$
By <orthogonality>, the $y_1$ term and all cross terms vanish. Using $I_0=\pi$ and $I_2=\pi/2$, the integral is
$$
\frac32\pi\left(\frac15+\frac{\varepsilon^2}{20}\right)
-\frac12\frac\pi2\frac{\varepsilon^2}{4}+O(\varepsilon^3)
=\boxed{\frac{3\pi}{10}+\frac{\pi}{80}\varepsilon^2+O(\varepsilon^3)}.
$$
The remainder can be integrated against this integrable weight in the regular expansion, so it retains the stated order.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
