= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the convention $\Delta G=+\delta^{(3)}$. The <Dirichlet Green function> for this <Poisson equation> satisfies, for each source point $\mathbf r'\in V$,
$$
\boxed{\Delta_{\mathbf r}G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf r-\mathbf r')\quad\text{in }V,
\qquad G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=0\quad\text{on }S}.
$$
It is a <harmonic function> away from its source, with a unit delta singularity there. Its <boundary condition> is homogeneous even when the original potential has nonzero boundary values; those values enter through the boundary integral. This sign convention is the one giving the plus signs in the formula requested in part (b).

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply <Green's second identity> to $\Phi(\mathbf r)$ and $G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')$, differentiating in $\mathbf r$:
$$
\int_V(\Phi\Delta G-G\Delta\Phi)\,dV
=\int_S\left(\Phi\frac{\partial G}{\partial n}-G\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial n}\right)dS.
$$
The volume terms become $\Phi(\mathbf r')-\int_VG\rho\,dV$. On the boundary $G=0$ and $\Phi=f$, so
$$
\boxed{\Phi(\mathbf r')=\int_V\rho(\mathbf r)G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')\,dV
+\int_S f(\mathbf r)\frac{\partial G}{\partial n}(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')\,dS}.
$$
Here $\partial/\partial n$ is the outward <normal derivative> in the integration variable. One may justify the identity at the singularity by excising a small sphere around $\mathbf r'$; its unit flux supplies the evaluation $\Phi(\mathbf r')$.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $\Delta G=+\delta^{(3)}$, the <fundamental solution of the Laplace equation> in three dimensions is
$$
\boxed{G_0(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=-\frac1{4\pi|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}}.
$$
Its sign follows from the flux: on a small sphere of radius $\varepsilon$, its outward radial <derivative> is $1/(4\pi\varepsilon^2)$, with total flux one.

Let $s=|\mathbf r'|\in(0,1)$ and put the image point at $\mathbf r'^*=\mathbf r'/s^2$. The <method of images> gives the <Dirichlet Green function inside a sphere>:
$$
\boxed{G_B(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=-\frac1{4\pi}
\left[\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}-\frac1{s|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'^*|}\right]}.
$$
The image lies outside the ball, so its potential is harmonic inside. On $|\mathbf r|=1$, the identity $|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|=s|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'^*|$ makes the two terms cancel. The original source retains unit strength. For a source at the centre, take the limit:
$$
\boxed{G_B(\mathbf r,0)=-\frac1{4\pi}\left(\frac1{|\mathbf r|}-1\right)}.
$$

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $\overline{\mathbf r'}=(x',y',-z')$ be reflection of the interior source across the equatorial plane. Using the ball <Green's function> from part (c), form
$$
\boxed{G_H(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')=G_B(\mathbf r,\mathbf r')-G_B(\mathbf r,\overline{\mathbf r'})}.
$$
Both terms vanish on the unit sphere. On the flat disk $z=0$, distances to reflected points coincide, and the same is true of their inversion images, so the difference is zero there as well. Only $\mathbf r'$ is inside the upper half-ball; the reflected source is in the lower half-ball, and both inversion images lie outside the full ball. Thus there is exactly one interior delta source, of the required strength.

Explicitly, with $s=|\mathbf r'|$ and stars denoting inversion in the unit sphere,
$$
\boxed{G_H=-\frac1{4\pi}\left[
\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|}-\frac1{s|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'^*|}
-\frac1{|\mathbf r-\overline{\mathbf r'}|}+\frac1{s|\mathbf r-\overline{\mathbf r'}^*|}
\right]}.
$$
This is the <Dirichlet Green function inside a hemisphere>, constructed from four image-source terms.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A sufficient <contour> setting for both results is a positively oriented, simple, closed, piecewise smooth <contour> $C$, with the function <holomorphic> on an open neighbourhood of $C$ and its entire interior. The <Cauchy integral theorem> then states
$$
\boxed{\oint_C f(z)\,dz=0}.
$$
If $z_0$ is strictly inside $C$, the <Cauchy integral formula> states
$$
\boxed{f(z_0)=\frac1{2\pi i}\oint_C\frac{f(z)}{z-z_0}\,dz}.
$$
The <contour> must not pass through $z_0$. Positive orientation means anticlockwise; reversing it reverses the sign of the <contour> integral. The holomorphy assumption throughout the interior excludes hidden singularities there.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The only denominator zero is $z_0=-1$, which lies strictly inside the given circle. The numerator is an <entire function>. Apply the <derivative> form of the <Cauchy integral formula> with $n=6$:
$$
\oint_C\frac{\sin z}{(z+1)^7}\,dz
=\frac{2\pi i}{6!}\left.\frac{d^6}{dz^6}\sin z\right|_{z=-1}.
$$
The sixth <derivative> is $-\sin z$, so its value at $-1$ is $\sin1$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\oint_C\frac{\sin z}{(z+1)^7}\,dz=\frac{\pi i\sin1}{360}}.
$$

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a positively oriented simple closed <contour>, the <residue theorem> states that the integral of a function holomorphic on and inside the <contour> except at finitely many isolated interior singularities is $2\pi i$ times the sum of its <residues>. There must be no singularity on the <contour>.

Factor the denominator as $(z^2+z+1)(z^2-z+1)$. For $R>1$, the upper semicircle encloses the two <simple poles>
$$
a=\frac12+\frac{i\sqrt3}{2},\qquad b=-\frac12+\frac{i\sqrt3}{2}.
$$
The other two <poles> are in the lower half-plane. Since $D'(z)=4z^3+2z=2z(2z^2+1)$,
$$
\operatorname{Res}_{a}g=\frac{e^{ia}}{2i\sqrt3\,a},\qquad
\operatorname{Res}_{b}g=-\frac{e^{ib}}{2i\sqrt3\,b}.
$$
Writing $h=\sqrt3/2$ and using $1/a=1/2-ih$, $1/b=-1/2-ih$, their numerator difference simplifies to
$$
\frac{e^{ia}}a-\frac{e^{ib}}b
=e^{-h}\left[\left(\frac12-ih\right)e^{i/2}+\left(\frac12+ih\right)e^{-i/2}\right]
=e^{-h}\left[\cos\frac12+\sqrt3\sin\frac12\right].
$$
Thus for every $R>1$ the closed-contour integral is
$$
\oint_{L_R\cup C_R}g(z)\,dz
=\frac\pi{\sqrt3}e^{-\sqrt3/2}\left[\cos\frac12+\sqrt3\sin\frac12\right].
$$
To remove the arc, on $C_R$ we have $|e^{iz}|=e^{-\operatorname{Im}z}\leq1$ and $|z^4+z^2+1|\geq R^4-R^2-1$. For sufficiently large $R$, the <complex line integral estimate> gives
$$
\left|\int_{C_R}g(z)\,dz\right|
\leq\frac{\pi R}{R^4-R^2-1}\longrightarrow0.
$$
The real-line integral is absolutely convergent. Its imaginary part is zero by oddness of $\sin x/(x^4+x^2+1)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\frac{\cos x}{x^4+x^2+1}\,dx
=\frac\pi{\sqrt3}e^{-\sqrt3/2}\left[\cos\frac12+\sqrt3\sin\frac12\right]}.
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
