= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A sufficient assumption is that $f$ is absolutely continuous, $f,f'\in L^1(\mathbb R)$, and $f(t)\to0$ at both infinities. <Integration by parts> in the <Fourier transform> gives
$$
\widetilde{f'}(\omega)
=\left[f(t)e^{-i\omega t}\right]_{-\infty}^{\infty}
+i\omega\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(t)e^{-i\omega t}\,dt
=\boxed{i\omega\widetilde f(\omega)}.
$$
The decay assumption removes the boundary term, and integrability makes both transforms ordinary convergent integrals. The same <derivative> identity extends to <tempered distributions>.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply the <Fourier transform of a derivative> twice to the <damped harmonic oscillator> equation. Under the corresponding convergence assumptions,
$$
(-\omega^2+2i\kappa\omega+\Omega^2)\widetilde y(\omega)=\widetilde f(\omega).
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\widetilde y(\omega)=\widetilde h(\omega)\widetilde f(\omega),\qquad
\widetilde h(\omega)=\frac1{\Omega^2-\omega^2+2i\kappa\omega}}.
$$
Since $\kappa,\Omega>0$, this denominator has no real zero: its imaginary part could vanish only at $\omega=0$, where its real part is positive. The transform therefore defines a regular response on the real frequency axis.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\beta=\sqrt{\Omega^2-\kappa^2}$, interpreted as $i\sqrt{\kappa^2-\Omega^2}$ in the <overdamped> case. Factoring the denominator gives
$$
\Omega^2-\omega^2+2i\kappa\omega=-(\omega-\omega_-)(\omega-\omega_+),\qquad
\boxed{\omega_\pm=i\kappa\pm\beta}.
$$
<Fourier inversion> and the <convolution theorem> give
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}G(t-\xi)f(\xi)\,d\xi},
$$
where
$$
G(t)=-\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\frac{e^{i\omega t}}{(\omega-\omega_-)(\omega-\omega_+)}\,d\omega.
$$
Thus the requested numerator is
$$
\boxed{s(\omega,t)=-\frac1{2\pi}e^{i\omega t}}.
$$
For all positive $\kappa,\Omega$, both <poles> lie in the upper half-plane. In the <overdamped> case their imaginary parts are $\kappa\pm\sqrt{\kappa^2-\Omega^2}$, both positive. At equality they merge into a double <pole>.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $t>0$, close the inverse-transform <contour> in the upper half-plane. There $|e^{i\omega t}|\leq1$; the rational factor is $O(|\omega|^{-2})$, so the arc integral vanishes. For $\Omega>\kappa$, write $\beta=\sqrt{\Omega^2-\kappa^2}>0$. The two <simple poles> are $i\kappa\pm\beta$, and the <residue theorem> yields
$$
\begin{aligned}
G(t)&=-i\left[\frac{e^{i\omega_+t}}{\omega_+-\omega_-}
+\frac{e^{i\omega_-t}}{\omega_--\omega_+}\right]\\
&=-i e^{-\kappa t}\frac{e^{i\beta t}-e^{-i\beta t}}{2\beta}
=\boxed{e^{-\kappa t}\frac{\sin(\beta t)}\beta}.
\end{aligned}
$$
This <causal Green function of a damped oscillator> has damped oscillations with envelope $e^{-\kappa t}$ and tends to zero as $t\to\infty$.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $\kappa>\Omega$, put $\eta=\sqrt{\kappa^2-\Omega^2}$, so $0<\eta<\kappa$ and the <poles> are $i(\kappa\pm\eta)$. Closing in the upper half-plane for $t>0$ again includes both <simple poles>. The <residue theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{G(t)=e^{-\kappa t}\frac{\sinh(\eta t)}\eta
=\frac{e^{-(\kappa-\eta)t}-e^{-(\kappa+\eta)t}}{2\eta}}.
$$
This <overdamped> response has no oscillation. Its slow tail is $e^{-(\kappa-\eta)t}/(2\eta)$, and it tends to zero because $\eta<\kappa$.

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At $\Omega=\kappa$, the inverse-transform denominator is $-(\omega-i\kappa)^2$. For $t>0$, its upper-half-plane double-pole <residue> is the <derivative> of the exponential:
$$
G(t)=-i\left.\frac{d}{d\omega}e^{i\omega t}\right|_{\omega=i\kappa}
=\boxed{t e^{-\kappa t}}.
$$
It is also the continuous limit of both answers above as $\beta\to0$ or $\eta\to0$. This <critically damped> response tends to zero with a linear factor multiplying the exponential.

In all three cases, for $t<0$ close the <contour> in the lower half-plane, where $e^{i\omega t}$ decays. There are no <poles> inside, so
$$
\boxed{G(t)=0\quad(t<0)}.
$$
At $t=0$ its continuous value is zero and $G'(0^+)=1$, producing the unit <derivative> jump required by $(D_t^2+2\kappa D_t+\Omega^2)G=\delta(t)$. Thus all three formulas are causal <Green's functions> and all decay to zero at late times.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For the critically damped case, causality reduces the <convolution> to
$$
y(t)=\int_0^\infty s e^{-\kappa s}\cos[\kappa(t-s)]\,ds.
$$
Although the forcing is not integrable on the full real line, this integral converges absolutely because the <Green's function> is integrable. Thus the calculation is valid directly; the corresponding <Fourier transform> can be interpreted as a <tempered distribution>.

Use the <complex exponential> and $\int_0^\infty s e^{-as}\,ds=a^{-2}$ for $\operatorname{Re}a>0$:
$$
y(t)=\operatorname{Re}\left[e^{i\kappa t}\int_0^\infty s e^{-\kappa(1+i)s}\,ds\right]
=\operatorname{Re}\left[\frac{e^{i\kappa t}}{\kappa^2(1+i)^2}\right].
$$
Since $(1+i)^2=2i$, the <convolution> response is
$$
\boxed{y(t)=\frac{\sin(\kappa t)}{2\kappa^2}}.
$$
This is the bounded steady response to the stated forcing acting for all time. Without an <initial condition> or the <convolution> selection, the general solution additionally contains $(C_1+C_2t)e^{-\kappa t}$. No switch-on at $t=0$ was specified, so zero initial displacement and <velocity> should not be imposed on this all-time forcing.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Split the <Cartesian second-rank tensor> into symmetric and antisymmetric parts:
$$
S_{ij}=\frac{T_{ij}+T_{ji}}2,\qquad A_{ij}=\frac{T_{ij}-T_{ji}}2.
$$
Define $u_k=\frac12\epsilon_{kij}T_{ij}$. The symmetric part contributes zero to this contraction. The <epsilon-delta identity> then gives
$$
\epsilon_{ijk}u_k=\frac12\epsilon_{ijk}\epsilon_{klm}T_{lm}
=\frac12(\delta_{il}\delta_{jm}-\delta_{im}\delta_{jl})T_{lm}
=\frac{T_{ij}-T_{ji}}2=A_{ij}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{T_{ij}=S_{ij}+\epsilon_{ijk}u_k,\qquad
S_{ij}=\frac{T_{ij}+T_{ji}}2,\qquad u_k=\frac12\epsilon_{kij}T_{ij}}.
$$
The symmetric part and the three independent antisymmetric components determine these quantities uniquely. Under proper rotations $u$ is a <vector>; under reflections it is an <axial vector>.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Apply the <symmetric gradient and curl decomposition> separately to the <electric field> and the <magnetic field>:
$$
\boxed{S^E_{ij}=\frac12(\partial_iE_j+\partial_jE_i),\qquad
S^B_{ij}=\frac12(\partial_iB_j+\partial_jB_i)}.
$$
The corresponding axial parts are
$$
\boxed{\mathbf u^E=\frac12\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac12\partial_t\mathbf B,
\qquad
\mathbf u^B=\frac12\nabla\times\mathbf B=\frac1{2c^2}\partial_t\mathbf E}.
$$
The second equalities use the vacuum <Maxwell equations>. Their <divergence> constraints also show that both symmetric <gradient> <tensors> are traceless.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiate $\mathbf u^E=\frac12\nabla\times\mathbf E$ with respect to time. The vacuum <Maxwell equations> give $\partial_t\mathbf E=c^2\nabla\times\mathbf B$, so
$$
\partial_t\mathbf u^E=\frac{c^2}{2}\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf B).
$$
The <curl of the curl identity> is $\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf B)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf B)-\nabla^2\mathbf B$. Since $\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial\mathbf u^E}{\partial t}=-\frac{c^2}{2}\nabla^2\mathbf B}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The same <Maxwell equations> give
$$
\partial_t\mathbf u^B=\frac12\nabla\times\partial_t\mathbf B
=-\frac12\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf E)=\frac12\nabla^2\mathbf E,
$$
where the last equality uses $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=0$. Combining with part (ii),
$$
\partial_t(u_i^E+u_i^B)=\frac12\nabla^2E_i-\frac{c^2}{2}\nabla^2B_i.
$$
The divergences of the symmetric gradients satisfy
$$
\partial_jS^E_{ij}=\frac12(\partial_i\partial_jE_j+\partial_j\partial_jE_i)=\frac12\nabla^2E_i,
\qquad \partial_jS^B_{ij}=\frac12\nabla^2B_i.
$$
Thus the local identity is $\partial_t(u_i^E+u_i^B)=\partial_j(S^E_{ij}-c^2S^B_{ij})$. Integrate over the fixed volume and apply the <divergence theorem> componentwise:
$$
\boxed{\frac\partial{\partial t}\int_V(u_i^E+u_i^B)\,dV
=\oint_A(S^E_{ij}-c^2S^B_{ij})\,dA_j}.
$$
Here $dA_j=n_jdA$ uses the outward <normal vector>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <divergence of a cross product> identity and the vacuum <Maxwell equations> imply
$$
\begin{aligned}
\nabla\cdot(\mathbf B\times\mathbf E)
&=\mathbf E\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf B)-\mathbf B\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf E)\\
&=\frac1{c^2}\mathbf E\cdot\partial_t\mathbf E+\mathbf B\cdot\partial_t\mathbf B\\
&=\partial_t\left(\frac{|\mathbf E|^2}{2c^2}+\frac{|\mathbf B|^2}{2}\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <divergence theorem> therefore gives the required balance with
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac12\left(\frac{|\mathbf E|^2}{c^2}+|\mathbf B|^2\right)},\qquad
\boxed{\frac\partial{\partial t}\int_V\lambda\,dV=\oint_A(\mathbf B\times\mathbf E)\cdot d\mathbf A}.
$$
An additive time-independent constant in $\lambda$ is immaterial. This is the vacuum <Poynting theorem> in the normalization of the given equations: equivalently $\partial_t\lambda+\nabla\cdot(\mathbf E\times\mathbf B)=0$. The order of the <cross product> accounts for the plus sign in the stated integral identity.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Choose <generalized coordinates> $q_i$ measured from a static <equilibrium point>, and assume a conservative system with no velocity-dependent forces. Keeping terms of degree two in displacements and velocities gives the <small oscillation> <Lagrangian>
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L=\frac12\dot{\mathbf q}^{\,T}A\dot{\mathbf q}-\frac12\mathbf q^TB\mathbf q}.
$$
Here $A$ is the positive-definite symmetric <mass> <matrix> evaluated at equilibrium, and $B$ is the symmetric <Hessian matrix> of the <potential energy> there. The linear potential term is zero by equilibrium, and an additive constant has been dropped. Coordinate-dependent corrections to the kinetic <matrix> contribute only at higher order.

The <Euler-Lagrange equations> are $A\ddot{\mathbf q}+B\mathbf q=0$. For a <normal mode> $\mathbf q=\mathbf v e^{i\omega t}$, they give the <generalized eigenvalue problem> $(B-\omega^2A)\mathbf v=0$. A nonzero <mode shape> exists exactly when
$$
\boxed{\det(B-\omega^2A)=0}.
$$
This is a degree-$n$ <polynomial> in $\omega^2$. At a stable equilibrium its roots are nonnegative; zero roots correspond to <zero modes>.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose positive $\phi$ so the bob is displaced to the right relative to the cart. Its <Cartesian coordinates> are
$$
X=x+L\sin\phi,\qquad Y=-L\cos\phi.
$$
Differentiation gives $\dot X^2+\dot Y^2=\dot x^2+2L\cos\phi\,\dot x\dot\phi+L^2\dot\phi^2$. Thus the exact <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> are
$$
T=\frac12(m+M)\dot x^2+ML\cos\phi\,\dot x\dot\phi+\frac12ML^2\dot\phi^2,
\qquad V=\frac12kx^2+MgL(1-\cos\phi).
$$
Retain quadratic terms, using $\cos\phi=1+O(\phi^2)$ in the kinetic cross term and $1-\cos\phi=\phi^2/2+O(\phi^4)$ in the potential. The <Lagrangian> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L=\frac12(m+M)\dot x^2+ML\dot x\dot\phi+\frac12ML^2\dot\phi^2
-\frac12kx^2-\frac12MgL\phi^2}.
$$
Its <Euler-Lagrange equations> give
$$
\boxed{(m+M)\ddot x+ML\ddot\phi+kx=0,\qquad
ML\ddot x+ML^2\ddot\phi+MgL\phi=0}.
$$
The kinetic cross term is essential: acceleration of the support couples the pendulum and cart. These equations define the <normal modes of a spring-mounted pendulum cart>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

With the stated parameters, the <mass> and restoring <matrices> are
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The <generalized eigenvalue problem> gives
$$
\det(B-\omega^2A)=(2-2\omega^2)(1-\omega^2)-\omega^4
=\omega^4-4\omega^2+2=0.
$$
Therefore the two normal <angular frequencies> are
$$
\boxed{\omega_{\mathrm{low}}=\sqrt{2-\sqrt2},\qquad
\omega_{\mathrm{high}}=\sqrt{2+\sqrt2}}.
$$
The second row of the modal equation is $-\omega^2x+(1-\omega^2)\phi=0$. It gives the <mode shapes>
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\omega_{\mathrm{low}}:&\quad (x,\phi)=a(1,\sqrt2)\cos(\omega_{\mathrm{low}}t+\delta),\\
\omega_{\mathrm{high}}:&\quad (x,\phi)=a(1,-\sqrt2)\cos(\omega_{\mathrm{high}}t+\delta).
\end{aligned}}
$$
Each <normal mode> has arbitrary small amplitude $a$ and phase $\delta$. The bob's horizontal displacement is $X=x+\phi$ to first order. Thus in the lower mode, bob and cart move in phase, with bob amplitude $1+\sqrt2$ times the cart amplitude. In the higher mode they move oppositely, because $X=(1-\sqrt2)x$; the bob amplitude is $\sqrt2-1$ times the cart amplitude. Its vertical displacement is quadratic in the angle and is absent at this order.

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $n=\operatorname{ord}(a)$, which is finite. A <group homomorphism> preserves powers and the identity, so
$$
f(a)^n=f(a^n)=f(e)=e'.
$$
If $r=\operatorname{ord}(f(a))$, divide $n$ by $r$: $n=qr+s$ with $0\leq s<r$. Then $e'=f(a)^n=f(a)^s$, and minimality of $r$ forces $s=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(f(a))\mid\operatorname{ord}(a),\qquad
\operatorname{ord}(f(a))\leq\operatorname{ord}(a)}.
$$
If $f$ is a <group isomorphism>, apply the same argument to its inverse: $\operatorname{ord}(a)\leq\operatorname{ord}(f(a))$. The two inequalities yield
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(f(a))=\operatorname{ord}(a)}.
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The two products are conjugate:
$$
a^{-1}(ab)a=ba.
$$
<Conjugation> by $a^{-1}$ is a <group automorphism>, so part (a) proves
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}(ab)=\operatorname{ord}(ba)}.
$$
Directly, cancellation gives $(ba)^r=a^{-1}(ab)^ra$ for every positive integer $r$. Thus a power of one product is the identity exactly when the same power of the other is the identity. This proves that <conjugation preserves element order>, even without the finiteness assumption.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The hypothesis implies $g^2=e$ for every $g\in G$, including the identity, so $g^{-1}=g$. For arbitrary $a,b\in G$, their product also equals its own inverse. The inverse-of-a-product rule therefore gives
$$
ab=(ab)^{-1}=b^{-1}a^{-1}=ba.
$$
Thus **$G$ is an <abelian group>**. Finiteness is not needed for this implication; it holds for every <group> in which each element squares to the identity.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume (I). Existence of a decomposition is part of the hypothesis. If $h_1h_2=k_1k_2$ are two decompositions, multiply on the left by $k_1^{-1}$ and on the right by $h_2^{-1}$ to obtain
$$
k_1^{-1}h_1=k_2h_2^{-1}.
$$
The left side belongs to $H_1$ and the right side to $H_2$, since they are <subgroups>. Their common value belongs to $H_1\cap H_2=\{e\}$, so $h_1=k_1$ and $h_2=k_2$. This proves uniqueness, hence (II).

Conversely, assume (II). Existence immediately gives $G=H_1H_2$. If $a\in H_1\cap H_2$, then
$$
a=a\cdot e=e\cdot a
$$
are two allowed factorizations, because the identity is in each <subgroup>. Uniqueness makes their first components equal, so $a=e$. Thus $H_1\cap H_2=\{e\}$ and (I) follows.

Therefore **(I) and (II) are equivalent**. This is an <exact factorization of a group>. For finite <groups> it gives $|G|=|H_1||H_2|$, but it does not by itself identify $G$ with a <direct product of groups>; multiplication between the two factors can be noncommutative.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The two diagonal generators are independent involutions, so
$$
H_1=\{\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2):\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2\in\{1,-1\}\},\qquad |H_1|=4.
$$
It is a <Klein four-group>. Write the swapping generator as $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$. Then $J^2=I$, so $H_2=\{I,J\}$ has order two. Moreover,
$$
J\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2)J
=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_2,\epsilon_1)\in H_1.
$$
Thus $J$ normalizes $H_1$. The set
$$
K=\{DJ^\eta:D\in H_1,\ \eta\in\{0,1\}\}
$$
is a <group>: its products reduce using
$$
(DJ^\eta)(D'J^\zeta)=D(J^\eta D'J^{-\eta})J^{\eta+\zeta},
$$
and inverses reduce in the same way. It contains both generating <subgroups>, so minimality gives $G\subseteq K$; conversely every displayed product is in $G$, giving $G=K$.

There are four diagonal <matrices> $D$ and four off-diagonal <matrices> $DJ$, all distinct. Consequently
$$
\boxed{|G|=8}.
$$
Also $H_1\cap H_2=\{I\}$, since $J$ is not diagonal, and every element of $G$ has the form $DJ^\eta\in H_1H_2$. This proves condition (I), hence the <exact factorization of a group>. Here $H_1$ is a <normal subgroup> and $G=H_1\rtimes H_2$ is a <semidirect product>. Its <matrices> are the two-dimensional <signed permutation matrices>, also realizing the <dihedral group> of the square.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <vector subspace> $W\subseteq\mathbb C^n$ is an <invariant subspace> for the <group representation> $D$ if
$$
\boxed{D(g)W\subseteq W\quad\text{for every }g\in G}.
$$
Because $g^{-1}$ is also in the <group> and $D(g)$ is invertible, the inclusion is equivalently $D(g)W=W$ for every $g$.

For $n>0$, the representation is an <irreducible representation> when its only invariant <vector subspaces> are
$$
\boxed{W=\{0\}\quad\text{and}\quad W=\mathbb C^n}.
$$
Thus no nonzero proper subspace is preserved by all representing <matrices>.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Every $D_2(g)$ is invertible because both inversion and the <conjugate transpose> preserve invertibility. For $g,h\in G$, the <group homomorphism> property of $D_1$ and reversal of multiplication under the adjoint give
$$
\begin{aligned}
D_2(gh)&=[D_1((gh)^{-1})]^\dagger\\
&=[D_1(h^{-1})D_1(g^{-1})]^\dagger\\
&=[D_1(g^{-1})]^\dagger[D_1(h^{-1})]^\dagger
=D_2(g)D_2(h).
\end{aligned}
$$
Also $D_2(e)=I$. Hence **$D_2$ is a <group representation>**. It is the <inverse-adjoint representation> of $D_1$; the inverse and adjoint each reverse product order, so together they preserve it.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the standard <Hermitian inner product> $\langle w,v\rangle=w^\dagger v$. Since $W$ is invariant under every $D_2(g)$, it is in particular invariant under
$$
D_2(g^{-1})=[D_1(g)]^\dagger.
$$
Let $v\in W^\perp$ and $w\in W$. Then
$$
\langle w,D_1(g)v\rangle
=\langle D_1(g)^\dagger w,v\rangle=0,
$$
because $D_1(g)^\dagger w\in W$ and $v$ is orthogonal to $W$. Thus $D_1(g)v$ is orthogonal to every $w\in W$, proving
$$
\boxed{D_1(g)W^\perp\subseteq W^\perp\quad(g\in G)}.
$$
Therefore $W^\perp$ is an <invariant subspace> for $D_1$. If $D_1$ is irreducible, $W^\perp$ is either $\{0\}$ or $\mathbb C^n$. In finite-dimensional <inner-product space>, $(W^\perp)^\perp=W$, so these alternatives force $W=\mathbb C^n$ or $W=\{0\}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{D_1\ \text{irreducible}\ \Longrightarrow\ D_2\ \text{irreducible}}.
$$
No unitarity of $D_1$ was assumed; the <inverse-adjoint representation> is exactly what makes this <orthogonal complement> argument work.
